Follow-up to ADR 0002 (spikes/windows-ip-helper): resolving the one open
anomaly the spike flagged but did not explain - each of the three
Notify*Change2 registrations fired its synthetic MibInitialNotification
callback TWICE (not once) when registered with family=AF_UNSPEC, on both the
25s and 60s runs. The ADR named a leading hypothesis and explicitly said it
was "not verified by re-running with explicit AF_INET/AF_INET6 separately,
which would be the obvious next check" - this is that check.

Change made: spikes/windows-ip-helper/main.go's three registerXNotify
functions now take a `family uint16` parameter (previously hardcoded
windows.AF_UNSPEC) and log it on every callback (`reqFamily=%d`). A new
`-split-family` flag makes main() register each of the three notification
types TWICE - once with AF_INET (2), once with AF_INET6 (23) - instead of
once with AF_UNSPEC (0), everything else (seed calls, observation duration,
cancellation) unchanged. Default behaviour (`-split-family` omitted) is
byte-for-byte the same as before: one AF_UNSPEC registration per type.

Command and real output (spikes/windows-ip-helper/run3-split-family-20s.txt,
full transcript kept alongside the code exactly as run1/run2 were):

    go vet ./...        # clean
    go build -o spike.exe .
    ./spike.exe -duration=20s -split-family=true

Result - the registration and callback section, verbatim:

    --- registering change notifications (split-family=true) ---
    NOTIFY iface: reqFamily=2 type=InitialNotification row=nil
    NotifyIpInterfaceChange(AF_INET): registered ok, handle=1789527597312
    NOTIFY addr: reqFamily=2 type=InitialNotification row=nil
    NotifyUnicastIpAddressChange(AF_INET): registered ok, handle=1789527662672
    NOTIFY route: reqFamily=2 type=InitialNotification row=nil
    NotifyRouteChange2(AF_INET): registered ok, handle=1789527666832
    NOTIFY iface: reqFamily=23 type=InitialNotification row=nil
    NotifyIpInterfaceChange(AF_INET6): registered ok, handle=1789527672528
    NOTIFY addr: reqFamily=23 type=InitialNotification row=nil
    NotifyUnicastIpAddressChange(AF_INET6): registered ok, handle=1789527682464
    NOTIFY route: reqFamily=23 type=InitialNotification row=nil
    NotifyRouteChange2(AF_INET6): registered ok, handle=1789527688112

    total notifications received: 6
      route          2
      iface          2
      addr           2

CONFIRMED, not just plausible: registering separately per family produced
exactly ONE initial-notification callback per (type, family) pair - 3 types
x 2 families = 6 total, each fired exactly once. This is the same total
count (6) as the original AF_UNSPEC runs, but now spread 1-per-family
instead of 2-on-one-handle. That is direct, positive evidence for the ADR's
leading hypothesis: passing AF_UNSPEC does not register "once, generically"
- it internally performs two registrations, one per address family, each of
which gets its own synthetic initial callback, both delivered through the
single handle/callback the caller sees. AF_UNSPEC is not a distinct wire
behaviour, it is sugar for "do this twice, once per real family."

This resolves ADR 0002's specific open question ("what causes the double
initial-notification per registration") but does NOT resolve the second half
of that same question, which this check was never designed to test: whether
a REAL (non-initial) notification would likewise be delivered twice under
AF_UNSPEC - e.g. would a single real address change on one interface fire
the callback once (correctly attributed to whichever family the address
actually belongs to) or twice (once per internal per-family registration,
regardless of which family the change was actually in)? This run made zero
real network changes (same passive-only safety boundary as the original
spike - see ADR 0002's Decision section) and none occurred naturally in the
20s window, so this remains open, now correctly scoped as "does the SAME
per-family duplication apply to real notifications" rather than "why do
initial notifications double" (answered).

Practical implication for a future WindowsLinkAdapter (5B): if it registers
with AF_UNSPEC (the natural choice, since internal/ports.InterfaceObservation
is address-family-agnostic and Linux's netlink adapter does not split by
family either), it must NOT assume "notification count == real event count."
Either de-duplicate in the adapter (the same category of problem
newOverflowReporter already exists to handle for netlink) or register
per-family explicitly and merge - the second option is now known to work
cleanly for at least the synthetic initial notification and costs one extra
handle per type (6 handles total instead of 3), which is cheap.

Verification:
- cd spikes/windows-ip-helper && go vet ./...: clean.
- go build -o spike.exe .: succeeds.
- ./spike.exe -duration=20s -split-family=true: ran to completion, exit 0,
  transcript preserved at run3-split-family-20s.txt.
- cd "<repo root>" && go vet ./...: still clean (nested module, same
  isolation property as before - confirmed again, not assumed, since other
  work in this repo continued concurrently).
- git status --porcelain at repo root: only spikes/windows-ip-helper/main.go
  (modified) and the new run3-split-family-20s.txt + rebuilt spike.exe are
  new/changed by this work; nothing under internal/, cmd/, desktop/,
  corpus/, ai/, rules/ touched.

Updated docs/product/adr/0002-windows-spike-findings.md's relevant section
to record this as a confirmed finding rather than an open question, and
narrowed the still-open question to the real-notification duplication case
specifically.

Still not done, same as before: no real (non-synthetic) notification of any
kind has been observed end-to-end on this machine; that remains deferred to
